<Site title="Privacy, Nordyg" description="What Nordyg connects to, what it stores, and what it does not do.">
  <article class="prose wrap">
    <h1>Privacy</h1>
    <p>Nordyg is a DNS client. Its whole job is to send questions to servers and show you the answers, so this page lists exactly which servers those are. It also serves as the privacy policy for the Mac App Store listing.</p>

    <h2>What the app connects to</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>The DNS resolvers you choose, over UDP, TCP, DNS over TLS, DNS over HTTPS or DNS over QUIC. The presets are public resolvers run by Cloudflare, Google, Quad9, NextDNS, AdGuard and Mullvad. You can add your own.</li>
      <li>The root servers and each zone's authoritative servers, when you run a trace or when DNSSEC validation needs keys the resolver did not provide.</li>
      <li>Registries' and registrars' RDAP servers, and for country-code domains without RDAP, <code>whois.iana.org</code> and the registry's WHOIS server, when you use registry mode.</li>
      <li><code>data.iana.org</code>, once per launch the first time registry mode is used, to refresh the list of RDAP servers.</li>
      <li><code>mta-sts.&lt;domain&gt;</code> over HTTPS, when the email report checks a domain that publishes an MTA-STS policy.</li>
      <li>The blocklist zones of Spamhaus, SpamCop and Barracuda, queried as ordinary DNS through your chosen resolver, in the email report.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Nordyg never contacts a server of mine. There is no account, no sign-in, no telemetry, no crash reporting and no update check. Updates come from the Mac App Store or from building a newer source tree. I cannot see what you look up, and I don't want to.</p>

    <h2>What the app stores</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Your query history, pinned queries and watches, as files inside the app's own sandbox container on your Mac.</li>
      <li>Custom resolvers you add.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Nothing leaves your Mac except the DNS, RDAP, WHOIS and HTTPS requests described above, sent to the servers you or the DNS point at.</p>

    <h2>The system resolver</h2>
    <p>Nordyg reads which resolvers macOS is configured to use, per network interface, so it can offer them in the picker and use them to look up DNS over HTTPS hosts. It does not send queries through the system resolver itself, except for the optional "what this Mac sees" comparison, which asks the system resolver for the same name you just queried.</p>

    <h2>This website</h2>
    <p>nordyg.com is a static site. It loads no analytics, no third-party scripts and no fonts from other hosts. The hosting provider keeps standard server logs, which I don't look at.</p>

    <h2>Contact</h2>
    <p>Questions about this? Ask in the <a href="https://github.com/n0rdy/nordyg/discussions">Discussions</a> on GitHub. The App Store listing is registered to my company, Do Not Panic AS, in Norway.</p>
